WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.300
-Our next guest is a four-time
Tony Award-winning actor,

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.634
playwright, and cultural icon
you know from his work

00:00:05.634 --> 00:00:07.300
in "Hairspray"
and "Mrs. Doubtfire."

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.534
is memoir, "I Was Better
Last Night," is available now.

00:00:10.534 --> 00:00:13.367
Please welcome to the show
Harvey Fierstein, everybody.

00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:15.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:23.567
♪♪

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:31.868
♪♪

00:00:31.868 --> 00:00:33.901
Harvey, I am so happy
you're here!

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:36.868
-I haven't seen people
in a very long time.

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:38.267
They haven't changed, though.

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:40.701
-They haven't changed.
-No, they look like people.

00:00:40.701 --> 00:00:42.400
-They still love you.
They still love you.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.434
-You know, 'cause I live
in a small, fictional town

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:46.501
in Connecticut,
so we don't see people there.

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:47.801
We have pictures of people.

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:49.968
-When did you -- Now,
have you always had a place

00:00:49.968 --> 00:00:52.601
in this small, fictional town,
or did you get it during COVID?

00:00:52.601 --> 00:00:54.267
-No, no, no.
I moved there in '84.

00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:56.234
-Oh, my God.
-I've been there a long time.

00:00:56.234 --> 00:00:57.434
-Oh, fantastic.
-Yeah.

00:00:57.434 --> 00:00:58.667
-Excellent.
-Long time.

00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:00.167
-What is it like, though,
when you walk

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.968
around the Theater District
in New York City?

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:03.901
Are people just so happy
to see you?

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:06.033
-Yeah, sort of.

00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:08.334
I think I've been there so long,
it's like,

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.133
"Oh, he's still alive?
We're okay."

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:11.834
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:13.734
-"It's gonna be fine.

00:01:13.734 --> 00:01:16.467
"Fierstein's still here.
We got a show to see."

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:17.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:17.901 --> 00:01:19.467
-Speaking of Broadway,
I know you're aware of this.

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:22.767
I have been percolating an idea
for a Broadway show

00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:25.467
that I'm gonna write, a musical
called "Cicada, Cicada."

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:26.901
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:26.901 --> 00:01:29.000
In it, Christine Baranski,
who's a soft "yes,"

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.300
plays a cicada,
who loves Broadway.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.567
And she comes to Broadway,

00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:36.000
and there's a New York
Mets-loving cockroach,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.133
and I keep saying
it's played by you.

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:39.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:42.234
And you sent these photos,
which I think means --

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:44.968
-[ Laughs ]
-I think means you're a yes!

00:01:44.968 --> 00:01:48.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.734
Are you in?

00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:53.767
-Seth, I'm what you call
a hard "yes."

00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:55.100
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.267
-A hard yes!
-I am a hard yes!

00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:58.868
-You know,
I heard about the story,

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:00.601
and I ran out
and got that costume.

00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:01.834
-Yeah.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:03.234
-And my brother
came over the house.

00:02:03.234 --> 00:02:05.501
I said, "Take a picture of me
in this costume for Seth."

00:02:05.501 --> 00:02:06.968
And we did.

00:02:06.968 --> 00:02:10.400
And then I turned on the TV,
'cause I had to sign books.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.434
And there was -- What is it?

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:14.767
"Let's Make a Deal."
-Uh-huh.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.300
And you know
they come in costumes?

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.367
-Yeah.

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:18.634
-And a guy's
wearing that costume.

00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:20.100
I said, "It's a sign.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.834
I got to write to Seth.
It's a sign."

00:02:21.834 --> 00:02:23.200
-Well, I'm so happy you're here.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.400
This is great 'cause Nathan Lane
has also shown interest.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.734
-Okay.
-And I think we're gonna

00:02:26.734 --> 00:02:28.434
pull this thing together.
-I think so.

00:02:28.434 --> 00:02:30.100
-Yeah.
Do we have a composer yet?

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.334
-We don't have a composer yet.
-I'll call Cyndi.

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:33.934
-Okay, that's great.
You call Cyndi,

00:02:33.934 --> 00:02:35.734
and I think we're gonna get
Tony number five for you.

00:02:35.734 --> 00:02:37.934
That's the goal here --
Tony number five.

00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:39.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:39.901 --> 00:02:43.534
Now, you are a man
who is full of great stories,

00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:45.133
and yet you just wrote
your memoir.

00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:48.234
What was the delay, and what
made you decide to do it?

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:50.734
-'Cause I cleared my desk
during COVID

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:52.834
and I did the laundry...

00:02:52.834 --> 00:02:55.767
and I had nothing to do.

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:57.100
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.834
And my agent said,
"Why don't you write a book?"

00:02:58.834 --> 00:03:00.267
And I said, "Okay."

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:01.434
[ Laughter ]

00:03:01.434 --> 00:03:03.033
Well, you know,
'cause on computers,

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.701
you don't even
have to use the paper.

00:03:04.701 --> 00:03:06.834
-Yeah.
-So I said -- So I sat down

00:03:06.834 --> 00:03:09.801
and I wrote the first story
that's in -- Not the foreword,

00:03:09.801 --> 00:03:12.067
but the story where I'm sitting
in second grade

00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:14.567
and they made me play --
In "Sleeping Beauty,"

00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:15.934
they made me play the King.
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:15.934 --> 00:03:17.300
-And I didn't want
to play the King.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.634
-No, you didn't want
to play that.

00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:20.033
-I didn't want to play the King.
-No.

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:21.667
-'Cause all he got was
a Reynolds Wrap crown.

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.167
-Yeah.
-And a bedspread

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:25.400
that you had to drag behind you.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.000
But my friend Philomena Marano,
she played the evil witch.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.067
-Uh-huh.
-And she got green skin

00:03:32.067 --> 00:03:33.567
and red lipstick.

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:35.434
And we went to
the penny-candy store

00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:36.801
and got her
those long, black nails.

00:03:36.801 --> 00:03:38.267
I wanted to play
the evil witch so bad.

00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:39.834
-Yeah.
-So bad.

00:03:39.834 --> 00:03:43.901
So I wrote the whole story.
And I said, "Does anybody care?"

00:03:43.901 --> 00:03:45.634
So I took the story
and I sent it to Philomena.

00:03:45.634 --> 00:03:47.100
-You're still in touch
with Philomena?

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.601
-We've been friends
since kindergarten.

00:03:48.601 --> 00:03:51.601
-Love it.
-Oh, I don't let friends go.

00:03:51.601 --> 00:03:52.968
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.968 --> 00:03:56.100
'Cause they can tell stories
on you. I'm telling ya.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.834
[ Laughter ]
So, I sent it to Philomena.

00:03:57.834 --> 00:04:00.801
She sent back the photograph
that's in the book!

00:04:00.801 --> 00:04:03.501
She had the photograph.
-Of you as a 7-year-old?

00:04:03.501 --> 00:04:05.334
-Me as a 7-year-old
in full drag.

00:04:05.334 --> 00:04:07.033
-Well, fantastic.
-For Halloween.

00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:08.400
-That's wonderful.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:09.901
Because, yeah, the following
Halloween, you were like,

00:04:09.901 --> 00:04:11.434
"I got to play the King
in the show.

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:13.567
You may be the Queen, but I'll
be the Queen for Halloween."

00:04:13.567 --> 00:04:16.334
-So I was --
I dressed up in female clothes,

00:04:16.334 --> 00:04:19.000
but I sort of messed up my face,
so I'd look like a zombie,

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.033
which made it
a socially acceptable drag.

00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:25.300
-Right.
-And she came as a bum.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.200
-There you go.
-In male drag.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.033
-So you talk a little bit about
what was socially acceptable,

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:33.067
the way people talked
about things back then.

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:37.133
You never thought about coming
to Broadway as a life.

00:04:37.133 --> 00:04:38.400
But people talked about you.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.033
You wanted to be artistic,
right?

00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:41.567
Like, "artistic"
was a word people used.

00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:43.567
-Yes, that was --
that meant "gay."

00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:45.601
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.601 --> 00:04:46.734
-That was polite for "gay."

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:49.167
-That was polite for "gay"
back in the day.

00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:50.667
-Before the '50s,
like, in the '40s,

00:04:50.667 --> 00:04:53.634
I think it was
"polite," I think.

00:04:53.634 --> 00:04:55.400
It might have been the word
for "gay."

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.667
[ Laughter ]
Then it became "artistic,"

00:04:57.667 --> 00:05:02.567
then it became "sensitive,"
and then it became...stuff.

00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:03.567
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:04.601
-And you were here
for all of it.

00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:05.901
You lived through all of it.

00:05:05.901 --> 00:05:07.634
-Yeah, exactly.
-So, you --

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:11.300
you talk in the book
about you auditioned for,

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:13.501
and were cast in,
an Andy Warhol play.

00:05:13.501 --> 00:05:15.467
-Well, I was
an artistic person.

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:17.067
I was working at
a community theater.

00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:21.901
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:26.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:26.567 --> 00:05:30.334
-So -- and I was working
at a community theater.

00:05:30.334 --> 00:05:33.767
And in "Showbiz," the paper,
there was an ad

00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:36.200
for Andy Warhol doing a play.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.367
I loved Warhol.

00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:40.100
Now, I didn't really like
the pop art yet,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.534
but he used to do
the illustrations

00:05:42.534 --> 00:05:43.367
for Bloomingdale's.

00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:44.467
-Yeah, beautiful.

00:05:44.467 --> 00:05:46.901
He was a wonderful
advertising artist.

00:05:46.901 --> 00:05:49.200
-Oh, unbelievable.
So I said, "Wait a second.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.267
I'm in a community theater.

00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:51.434
I'm like an actor."

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:53.267
So I asked my friend,
Irene Stein,

00:05:53.267 --> 00:05:54.868
she took a photograph of me.

00:05:54.868 --> 00:05:57.067
I had to develop it
in my basement

00:05:57.067 --> 00:05:58.968
'cause I was artistic.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:58.968 --> 00:06:02.968
And then I wrote up a résumé,
and I stapled it together.

00:06:02.968 --> 00:06:05.267
And all of a sudden,
I had an 8x10 and a résumé,

00:06:05.267 --> 00:06:07.634
and off I went
to meet Andy Warhol.

00:06:07.634 --> 00:06:09.567
-And?
-I got in the show.

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:12.200
-That's fantastic.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.200
-So all of a sudden,
I was "a actor."

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.367
-That's wonderful.
So, that was it.

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:18.534
-I never planned
on being "a actor."

00:06:18.534 --> 00:06:21.400
And then they put me in a dress,
so I felt very at home.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.901
-That's wonderful.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:26.067
Speaking of your relationship
to Broadway --

00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:28.467
and before we move on
to some of your film work --

00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:31.400
you're working on "Funny Girl"
right now, which is so exciting.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:34.300
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.467
We did it first in London.
-Mm-hmm.

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:38.834
-Oh, Sheridan Smith
was wonderful.

00:06:38.834 --> 00:06:40.834
We did it in London.
It was a big hit.

00:06:40.834 --> 00:06:42.801
And then it ran for a year,
and then it toured,

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:45.634
and then we were supposed
to come here, And then COVID.

00:06:45.634 --> 00:06:46.801
[ Laughter ]

00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:48.067
So now we're finally
getting to do it,

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:49.901
and it's very exciting.
It's in rehearsal.

00:06:49.901 --> 00:06:51.234
Beanie said she's coming here.

00:06:51.234 --> 00:06:52.234
-Yeah, we're gonna
have Beanie here.

00:06:52.234 --> 00:06:53.667
I can't wait, I can't wait.

00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:55.934
I can't wait to see it.
-And Jane Lynch.

00:06:55.934 --> 00:06:57.133
-What a cast.

00:06:57.133 --> 00:06:58.434
[ Cheers and applause ]
What a cast.

00:06:58.434 --> 00:06:59.834
-Yeah.

00:06:59.834 --> 00:07:01.367
-You talk about a person
who is really special to you,

00:07:01.367 --> 00:07:02.601
and I think special
to all of us,

00:07:02.601 --> 00:07:04.734
even those who didn't meet him.

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:06.133
Robin Williams.

00:07:06.133 --> 00:07:08.267
You played --
-You just want me to cry.

00:07:08.267 --> 00:07:09.300
-No, I don't.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:10.801
I promise,
I don't want you to cry.

00:07:10.801 --> 00:07:13.534
But I would like you to tell
the very funny story

00:07:13.534 --> 00:07:16.234
of how that role came to you.

00:07:16.234 --> 00:07:17.434
Because oftentimes people

00:07:17.434 --> 00:07:20.467
see you doing fantastic
in front of an audience,

00:07:20.467 --> 00:07:21.934
and they think,
"I have to have this person,"

00:07:21.934 --> 00:07:23.000
but that's not what happened.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.300
-That's not how life is.
-No.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.334
-No.
Especially for comedians.

00:07:26.334 --> 00:07:28.267
-Yeah.
-So I was asked to do --

00:07:28.267 --> 00:07:31.100
Lily Tomlin asked me
to do a benefit with her

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:32.667
and Robin Williams

00:07:32.667 --> 00:07:35.567
to raise money for this movie
they were -- a documentary.

00:07:35.567 --> 00:07:37.267
And I thought I just had
to introduce them,

00:07:37.267 --> 00:07:38.734
but when I got there, they said,
"No, no, no,

00:07:38.734 --> 00:07:40.434
you have to do
a half-hour of stand-up."

00:07:40.434 --> 00:07:42.734
I said, "But I ain't got
no half-hour of stand-up."

00:07:42.734 --> 00:07:44.267
And they said,
"Well, just say something."

00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:45.868
So I looked at my bag.

00:07:45.868 --> 00:07:47.467
I was filming a movie
with Alicia Witt,

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:49.434
and I had no clothes with me,

00:07:49.434 --> 00:07:51.767
but I had, like, plaid shirts
and dungarees or whatever.

00:07:51.767 --> 00:07:55.400
I said, "I'll do
a lesbian fashion show."

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:57.601 --> 00:07:58.901
"This is San Francisco.

00:07:58.901 --> 00:08:00.634
This is gonna be gangbusters."

00:08:00.634 --> 00:08:01.934
-Sure, you thought
you had a hit.

00:08:01.934 --> 00:08:05.701
-I thought, "Oh,
this is gonna go over big."

00:08:05.701 --> 00:08:08.367
They had to send guards
to get me out of town.

00:08:08.367 --> 00:08:11.400
[ Laughter ]
And I was so bad,

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.501
and they hated me so much,
they just kept booing.

00:08:13.501 --> 00:08:14.901
They couldn't even
hear the jokes.

00:08:14.901 --> 00:08:16.033
They just were booing.

00:08:16.033 --> 00:08:18.033
And Robin stood in the wings,
watching,

00:08:18.033 --> 00:08:19.734
'cause as a stand-up comic,

00:08:19.734 --> 00:08:21.601
he knew what it was like
to bomb like that.

00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:23.067
-Yes.
-And I just kept looking over,

00:08:23.067 --> 00:08:24.167
and he's laughing.

00:08:24.167 --> 00:08:25.968
And in the other wings,
Lily is laughing,

00:08:25.968 --> 00:08:27.467
and I said,
"Oh, this is great."

00:08:27.467 --> 00:08:30.334
So I lived through that night.
I mean, Lily at one point said,

00:08:30.334 --> 00:08:32.567
"Harvey, could you bring me
a glass of water?"

00:08:32.567 --> 00:08:33.701
She was out onstage.

00:08:33.701 --> 00:08:35.133
So I came out
with a glass of water.

00:08:35.133 --> 00:08:37.300
The newspaper said I tried
to upstage her

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:39.734
by bringing her
a glass of water.

00:08:39.734 --> 00:08:42.100
[ Laughter ]
That's how bad it went.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:43.968
Anyway, the next day,
I got a phone call saying,

00:08:43.968 --> 00:08:45.267
"You want to play my brother?"

00:08:45.267 --> 00:08:46.667
-And that's how
"Mrs. Doubtfire" came to be.

00:08:46.667 --> 00:08:49.300
-Unbelievable.
Well, what a special person.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:50.567
And you are, too.

00:08:50.567 --> 00:08:52.801
And let me tell you,
having you here tonight --

00:08:52.801 --> 00:08:54.234
I mean, obviously,
we're gonna work together a lot

00:08:54.234 --> 00:08:55.601
on "Cicada, Cicada"...
[ Laughter ]

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:58.200
...but having you here tonight
was deeply special to me.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.267
Thank you so much.

00:08:59.267 --> 00:09:00.367
And congratulations
on your memoir.

00:09:00.367 --> 00:09:01.467
-Thank you for having me.

00:09:01.467 --> 00:09:02.834
-You guys,
that's Harvey Fierstein.

00:09:02.834 --> 00:09:06.300
His memoir, "I Was Better
Last Night," is on sale now.

